
The "Post-Perception" Movement: Organizational Structure and Artistic Classifications
A Study on the Framework of a Reference Book for a Contemporary Artistic Movement
Ibrahim Shalabi Conceptual Artist and Founder of the "Post-Perception" Movement
________________________________________
Abstract
This academic research provides an analytical description of the organizational structure of the book Post-Perception (Volume II of the Color Box series), the definitive reference establishing this contemporary artistic-philosophical movement.
The study reviews the six-fold classification of the movement’s body of work (33 works distributed across 6 categories) and analyzes the internal logic linking them. It demonstrates how the book's organizational hierarchy reflects the conceptual evolution of the movement—from visual deconstruction to spiritual contemplation. The research aims to provide a reference tool for scholars, critics, and students to understand the movement's architecture and methodology.
________________________________________
I. Introduction: The Necessity of an Organizational Framework
When establishing a new artistic movement, producing scattered works is insufficient. These works must be presented within a "systematic framework" that reflects their interrelationships, clarifies their conceptual trajectory, and facilitates the researcher’s understanding of the movement's internal logic.
The book Post-Perception provides this framework through a "six-fold classification" encompassing 33 works. This taxonomy is not arbitrary; rather, it reflects an organic evolution within the movement's structure—moving from foundational inquiries to final spiritual reflections.
________________________________________
II. The General Structure of the Book
2.1 Main Sections
The book consists of three primary parts:
•	Part I: The Conceptual and Philosophical Framework
o	Chapters 1–3: Historical evolution of perception, definition of the movement, and the Founding Manifesto.
o	Objective: Establishing the philosophical and theoretical vision.
•	Part II: Applied Study of Artworks
o	Chapters 4–8: Analysis of 33 works across 6 classifications.
o	Objective: Applying theoretical principles to concrete works.
•	Part III: Practice, Theorization, and Global Impact
o	Chapters 9–13: Rooting the movement, practical guide, comparative studies, and the future.
o	Objective: Expanding the movement's horizon and linking it to a global context.
2.2 Taxonomic Methodology
The six-fold classification is based on conceptual—rather than purely chronological—criteria. Each work is categorized according to its functional role in developing the movement's discourse. Consequently, a recently produced work may belong to a "Foundational" category, while an older work might be "Experimental."
